{
  "research_problems": [
    {
      "text": "Investigating design criteria for software architecture and development processes to enable Continuous Experimentation in complex cyber-physical systems like self-driving vehicles.",
      "original_text": "This work investigates design criteria for the software architecture and the corresponding software development and deployment process for complex cyber-physical systems, with the goal of enabling Continuous Experimentation as a way to achieve continuous software evolution."
    },
    {
      "text": "Addressing challenges in adopting Continuous Experimentation in safety-critical cyber-physical systems due to hardware constraints, safety regulations, and data transfer scalability.",
      "original_text": "In the case of safety-critical cyber-physical systems there are still many challenges to be overcome in order for Continuous Experimentation to be adopted, as shown in our previous study 5 . These challenges are mostly related to the constraints in terms of resources and safety-critical regulations that the software must always obey. They can be summarized with: hardware constraints, industries usually provide automotive platforms with just enough processing power due to the costs of mass production, which means that it can be hard to find the additional computation memory bandwidth that is needed for additional software to run in piggyback mode on a car if it was not built for that task; safety constraints, the software must guarantee that the performances are always compliant with safety regulations, otherwise the car is considered illegal on public roads; scalability of data transfer, as the sensors used for self-driving vehicles generate a vast amount of data each second (a test run with our vehicles can produce around 300MB s, mostly due to camera and radars data streams), a sound strategy is needed to manage how the vehicles will upload their data back to the manufacturers research facilities when needed, especially considering that these vehicles may be present in great numbers in metropolitan areas."
