{
  "research_problems": [
    {
      "text": "The challenge of eliciting and documenting interactions with the operational context of cyber-physical systems (CPS), especially in dynamic CPS networks where systems can join or leave spontaneously, making it impossible to predict operational contexts at design time.",
      "original_text": "For cyber- physical systems acting in system networks, this task becomes challenging since these networks can change at runtime, because individual systems can leave or join a network spontaneously. Hence, it becomes impossible to predict at design time the operational contexts at runtime."
    },
    {
      "text": "The difficulty in documenting functional dependencies and emergent functionality in CPS networks due to dynamic composition and runtime changes, which existing static context and contract-based approaches cannot handle.",
      "original_text": "In CPS networks, interactions between CPS and their operational context give rise to several challenges. One challenge we will particularly emphasize within this paper is functional dependencies of collaborating systems such as the CACC. These functional dependencies emerge from the interplay of independent systems. This contrasts with other paradigms where functional dependencies are typically designed top-down."
    },
    {
      "text": "The inadequacy of existing approaches that assume static and fully known operational contexts at design time, which fail to support the dynamic and emergent nature of CPS networks.",
      "original_text": "As can be seen from the discussions in the previous sections, accounting for static context information during system development has been achieved in the past. However, as can also be seen, the dynamically changing composition of CPS networks at runtime makes it impossible to predict the specific nature of the operational context of every individual CPS. In consequence, existing approaches fall short of leveraging functionality of a CPS network, which means that functional dependencies remain covert."
    },
    {
      "text": "In CPS development, the closed world assumption no longer holds, as CPS networks are dynamically created at runtime, leading to heterogeneous CPS networks composed of systems from different manufacturers and capabilities, making static system boundaries and context assumptions invalid.",
      "original_text": "In CPS development, however, the closed world assumption no longer holds. Among other things, the functionality in a CPS network is not restricted to individual systems but can also emerge from their interplay (e.g., 11 , 14 ). CPS make use of sophisticated communication networks (e.g., wireless ad-hoc connections) to access each others functionality. Albeit other forms of coupling between CPS are conceivable (e.g., sharing sensor data or domain observations at runtime), accessing functionality of individual CPS within the CPS network means that a cyber-physical SUD interacts not only with other systems in the same car, but possibly also within other cars. This collaboration is often unbeknownst to the individual systems (see 9 for a detailed account of such an interplay). As we have outlined in Section I, the key difference to embedded systems lies in the fact that in CPS, the network can be dynamically created at runtime. In consequence, the specific external CPS an individual CPS interacts with at runtime dynamically changes as well. This may result in heterogeneous CPS networks 18 , where the CPS network is composed of individual CPS of different manufacturers and different functional capabilities."
    },
    {
      "text": "The operational context and system boundary of CPS networks emerge from overlapping contexts of individual CPS and change dynamically at runtime, making static context perceptions inapplicable.",
      "original_text": "Hence, in this example, the system boundary and the operational context of the CPS network emerges from the union of overlapping contexts of the individual CPSs. Moreover, since networks are created dynamically, the system boundary is subject to change at runtime. This is because a CPS might be added or removed from the network (i.e. a car drives out of communication range with the smart road sign or other cars), thereby adding or removing functionality, and hence impacting the overall functionality of the CPS network. Obviously, a static perception of a systems context is not applicable. As outlined in 50 , closed-world assumption fails."
    },
    {
      "text": "The operational context of a CPS must be defined considering the CPS network scope and specific intended use in concrete operational situations, requiring a view-centric documentation approach focusing on functional interactions and dependencies within the CPS network.",
      "original_text": "Instead, the operational context of a CPS must be defined under consideration of the scope of the CPS network, and furthermore, dependent on the specific intended use in a concrete operational situation (see, e.g., 11 ). Albeit each CPS can be regarded individually using the same principles as embedded systems, the cyber-physical SUD and its context cannot be separated by a single system boundary, as the boundary changes with the runtime properties of the CPS network. Therefore, we propose documenting the context for functionally interdependent CPS in CPS networks in a view- centric way, i.e. from the view of functional interaction. The context for each function available within the CPS network consists of the overlap of the context of individual CPS. The concern expressed in the view is hence the assumed functional dependency between the types of CPS and the functions they make accessible to the CPS network."
    },
    {
      "text": "The challenge of modeling and documenting the functional interplay and emergent functionality in CPS networks, exemplified by cooperative adaptive cruise controls (CACCs) forming dynamic platoons, requiring explicit documentation of assumptions about context functions and interactions to leverage emergent network functionality.",
      "original_text": "In Figure 3 cooperative ACCs (CACCs, cf. 51 ) are shown, which extend common ACCs by building platoons of cars dynamically (i.e. at runtime), in order to coordinate speed and lane changes in dense traffic. In Figure 3, the CACCs perform some internal functions, provide functions to other CACCs in the network, and rely on functions of other CACCs. For instance, each CACC publishes its own vehicles current and desired speed across the network. This allows other CACCs to adapt their speed accordingly. In doing so, forming and coordinating platoons becomes an emergent functionality of the CPS network made up of several CACCs. This emergent functionality, does not belong to one single CACC, but is deployed within the CPS network. Hence, to leverage emergent functionality, the individual CACCs functions of altering and publishing their own speeds must be taken into account. This is done by documenting the operational context of individual CACCs."
    },
    {
      "text": "The need for a view-centric context modeling approach that uses simplified UML activity diagrams to document functions and interactions within individual CPS and their operational contexts, enabling merging of views to investigate emergent functionality and making explicit assumptions about functional dependencies in CPS networks.",
      "original_text": "As can be seen in Figure 3, we use simplified UML activity diagrams to document functionality of each CACC. Hence, in Figure 3, the rounded-corner rectangles represent functions of the SUD and double-ended solid arrows document interactions between them. This yields a view of the functionality within a system; one activity diagram is used for each CPS within the CPS network. Similarly, the functionality within the operational context of each CACC is also documented using rounded-edge UML activities. Again, double-ended solid arrows represent interaction between the context functionality the individual CACCs can make use of. This yields another view of the functionality within the context. To combine the views and therefore leverage functional dependencies between individual CPS and the CPS network, double-ended dashed are used to symbolize an interaction with the context. It is to note that the functionality within the context is not explicitly designed by a developer but emerges through context interactions of several CPS (see Sections I and III.B). Therefore, the context functions depicted in Figure 3 represent assumptions on type level about how the interaction will take place at runtime on instance level. Using this simple visualization of functionality within the systems as well as the context, these assumptions are now made explicit. During development of individual CPS, context views of each individual CPS under development (i.e. CACC1 and CACC2 in Figure 3) can be merged to generate and investigate emergent functionality within the CPS network. This fosters the documentation of assumptions about functionality within the operational context of any individual CPS under development. This approach hence may alleviate the limitations of the closed-world assumption inherent in embedded systems development by showing what the mutual influence of two or more CPS on the same physical property in the context is (e.g., platoon speed in Figure 3)."
    },
    {
      "text": "The view-centric context modeling approach allows building purpose-specific views for documenting design rationales and analyzing functionality of individual CPS and CPS networks, supporting concerns such as safety or variability, and differentiating views centered on individual systems, the CPS network, or overarching concerns.",
      "original_text": "In detail, such a view-centric context modeling approach allows for building purpose specific views for documenting design rationales as well as for analyzing the functionality of the system under development within its context. This is fostered by differentiating between views specific for the individual system within the CPS network (which is the context of the individual system) and views for the CPS network and its context. Furthermore, not necessarily must a view be centered on the system or the system network as a whole but can, for instance, focus on overarching concerns 33 , such as safety or variability."
    },
    {
      "text": "The challenge of modeling functional design in CPS networks by distinguishing system functions, context functions, and CPS network functions, including collaborative system and context functions, local context functions, and pure context functions, to accurately depict functional interplay and dependencies.",
      "original_text": "In modern system development, functions are designed to be shared between the different embedded systems (e.g., 52 , 53 , 54 ). Hence, modeling of a functional design needs to allow for separation between system functions and context functions (see 51 ). The term system function refers to a logical function, which is part of the context subject. Context functions refer to logical functions, which are available to the context subject, but are part of context objects. For example, an automotive adaptive cruise control system makes use of braking functions provided by the electronic stability control to maintain a safe distance to preceding vehicles. We applied the context modeling framework 38 to an existing functional modeling technique, whose meta model is given in Figure 4. As can be seen, functions are structurally connected and are defined by a specific function behavior. Figure 5 shows the extensions made to the meta model shown in Figure 4 to account for the different types of functions in CPS networks. It is no longer sufficient to distinguish only between system and context function; CPS network functions must also be considered. CPS network functions comprise collaborative functions that are provided by the various CPS of the CPS network. Depending on which system in the network is considered the context subject (i.e. the subject, upon which the context acts, see 55 ), these collaborative functions are either collaborative system functions (i.e. Exchange Speed in Figure 3) or collaborative context functions (i.e. Control Platoons Speed in Figure 3). Beside collaborative system functions, CPS also have traditional system functions with no collaborative properties, which are referred to as pure system functions (i.e. Determine Desired Speed in Figure 3). Furthermore, the context of CPS also comprises traditional context functions or non-collaborative context functions. This means functions that are not provided by another collaborative system in the same system network, but by some other context entity. In case this context entity is part of the same super system as the context subject, we refer to this function as a local context function, otherwise these functions are considered pure context functions. These distinctions between context functions of the system, which are part of the same super system and context functions, which are part of other systems within the system network, must be made as those functions are typically not constantly available and must be considered volatile, less reliable, less dependable, and less trustworthy."
    },
    {
      "text": "The benefit of view-centric context modeling includes structured documentation of system and context functionalities, enabling analyses such as safety, compatibility, and impact analyses, and serving as a prerequisite for design approaches like contract-based design.",
      "original_text": "In addition to aiding the structured documentation of system and context functionalities, the functional modeling approach outlined above fosters several analyses. These analyses include safety analyses, compatibility analyses with neighboring systems, impact analyses, etc. In other words, this structured documentation of system and context functionality of a CPS network can be used as a prerequisite for design approaches, such as contract-based design."
    },
    {
      "text": "View-centric context modeling supports compatibility checking of a CPS within its network by clarifying non-collaborative and collaborative context functions, ensuring proper cooperation with embedded systems and other CPS, and verifying that the CPS and CPS network achieve their goals.",
      "original_text": "One benefit of the application of view-centric context modeling is the use for compatibility checking of the single CPS with the other partaking CPS and with the CPS network. On one hand, compatibility checking between the system and its context is supported as it is clear what the non-collaborative context is, and how the collaborative context differs from it. For the CACC case example, this means that it must be checked whether the CACC is compatible with other CPS and locally connected (e.g., through traditional CANBUS) embedded systems. This can aid in ascertaining whether, for example, the CACC can properly cooperate with the engine control unit to ensure functionality of the context function Regulate Engine Torque or, for another example, if the chosen weather service provides a function Broadcast Weather Information that is compatible with the system function Receive Weather Information. On the other hand, it becomes clear that the functionalities Reduce Speed and Determine Optimal Speed depend on collaboration with other CPSs in the same CPS network. Hence, compatibility of these functions with the CPS network must be ensured. In this case, compatibility checks must take both directions into account. First, it must be ensured the CACC under development can achieve its own goals and is provided with all necessary information to correctly accelerate and decelerate its own car. Second, it must be ensured the entire CPS network can properly work so the overall goal of the CPS network is achieved (i.e. the platoon moves with optimal speed and minimal distance without abrupt braking or collisions)."
    },
    {
      "text": "View-centric context modeling enables definition and validation of CPS network safety goals by identifying hazards from collaboration, making safety claims about system network functions, and considering reliability and interplay of different CPS implementations and communication channels.",
      "original_text": "Through safety analyses, such as the FMEA 56 , potential failures are identified and safety goals and safety requirements are defined as mitigations to prevent the occurrence of these failures 17 . For safety analyses, a certain closed-world must be assumed for the simple reason that absolute safety is usually unattainable. Hence, safety claims typically argue a certain level of safety, given some explicitly assumed operational context at runtime. In the engineering of CPS, which collaborate in a CPS network, the fact that the closed world assumption fails impairs the ability to make safety claims about functionally dependent CPS. For example, safety engineers might identify rear-end collisions as a potential hazard and will, therefore, check whether the CPS as specified can reach a state where such a collision is possible. Using view-centric context modeling, which considers the different aspects of functionality belonging to the CPS as well as to the CPS network, hazards from the collaboration can be detected during safety analyses by considering the overlap of system functions. Moreover, safety claims can be made for system network functions. By making assumptions about functional dependencies using the approach proposed above limits the open world enough to make certain statements about the safety of the CPS network (albeit this may be too much of a simplification). For instance, in Figure 6, the safety engineer can easily identify the system network function Avoid Collisions. One aspect of the safety analysis will be to investigate whether this function properly ensures the avoidance of collisions. In this case, it can easily be perceived that the proper functioning of Avoid Collisions does not only depend on a single CPS (i.e. the CPS under development) but also on other CPSs in the CPS network. The CPSs in the CPS network might be CACCs of the same type (i.e. other instances of the system under development) or CACCs by different vendors. Ensuring compliance with the safety requirement must, among others, consider the possibility of error prone implementations of other CACCs as well as the loss of communication channels at runtime (e.g., due to poor wireless signal reception). Hence, safety requirements might deal with the reliability of communication and safe behavior to be exhibited in case of communication loss but also with standardization or runtime compatibility checks to ensure proper interplay of different CACCs."
