    {
      "text": "Software architects face challenges in designing PaaS cloud applications due to the complexity of PaaS services, variability of the environment, and quality conflicts in architectural tactics, making it difficult to predict application quality without time-consuming prototype implementation.",
      "original_text": "Despite the emergence of architectural tactics and patterns usable for the design of PaaS cloud applications, it is challenging for the software architects to navigate in a rich set of offered services, variability of the PaaS cloud environment and quality conflicts in existing tactics, which makes it almost impossible to predicts the overall application quality without time-consuming implementation of application prototypes."
    },
    {
      "text": "Current performance prediction tools and modeling approaches are inadequate for PaaS environments due to hidden platform complexity, multi-tenancy, and frequent updates, limiting their applicability for early quality evaluation of PaaS applications.",
      "original_text": "Most of the performance prediction tools work well in environments with known parameters of employed resources (CPU, memory, I O operations, network bandwidth) and controlled distribution of resources among multiple con- sumers, but experience problems in case of PaaS environment. In a nutshell, the employment of the PaaS cloud hinders the applicability of the current modeling and simulation ap- proaches for performance evaluation because of the following challenges associated with the PaaS cloud: Hidden complexity of the platform Complex func- tionality, high availability, good scalability and elasticity, are all accomplished by complex service architecture of the PaaS cloud services that typically include multiple computation nodes, shared load balancers, partitioning of data, etc. It is impossible to model such a service ecosystem from scratch using current tools, due to the lack of publicly available information about the inner architecture of the PaaS services, and hidden resource limits of the infrastructure unavailable to the architect. Multi-tenant environment Multiple PaaS cloud ser- vices give us an option to host the application in shared environment, where the application is strictly isolated from other applications running on the same server (due to security reasons), but sharing the computational re- sources of the server. In that case we pay for dedicated environment, which leads internally to provisioning of dedicated virtual servers managed by the cloud operator. We can measure performance of the server and built model of the application using tools as if it was hosted 2017 IEEE International Conference on Software Architecture 978-1-5090-5729-0 17 31.00 2017 IEEE DOI 10.1109 ICSA. on a dedicated IaaS virtual machine. But in this case, we are missing some side effects, caused by service architecture, which is not comprised of dedicated hosting servers only, but of shared network infrastructure, load balancers, etc., which may affect validity of the results due to the presence of noisy neighbors. Updates of the environment Cloud providers are heavily investing in servicing their infrastructures with hardware upgrades and software optimization of their services on daily or weekly basis. Therefore, even if we would decide to build a mathematical model of the plat- form by conducting a set of comprehensive performance measurements, its validity would be very limited and we would need to re-run the measurements on weekly basis and rebuild the model."
