      "description": "A platform for architectural performance modelling that supports simulation and extensions for architectural optimisation and new qualities.",
      "original_text": "Palladio Component Model (PCM). [18] is the platform used in this paper to model architecture performance characteristics. Palladio was selected as it is freely available, supports simulation, provides a familiar \u0002UML-like\u0002 interface for model creation, and has the flexibility to incorporate extensions such as architectural optimisation tools [8, 13], new qualities [21], and new kinds of systems [23]."
    },
    {
      "name": "PerOpteryx",
      "description": "An automated design space exploration tool for PCM that uses genetic algorithms to search for Pareto-optimal candidates optimizing multiple quality attributes such as performance and cost.",
      "original_text": "PerOpteryx. [13] is an automated design space exploration tool for PCM, capable of exploring many degrees of freedom. PerOpteryx starts with a PCM instance and a set of design decision models that describe how the architecture can be changed. Automated search over this design space is performed using a genetic algorithm. For each generation in the search, a Palladio instance is generated and analysed to evaluate quality attributes such as performance and cost. PerOpteryx is capable of optimising multiple quality attributes by searching for Pareto-optimal candidates."
    },
    {
      "name": "Static Taint Analysis",
      "description": "A security analysis technique that models the propagation of taint (representing potential attack reach) through system components to identify vulnerabilities related to data flow.",
      "original_text": "Static taint analysis is a simple automatic security analysis approach. Taint analysis is not a perfect model of security, but is a popular technique for identification of architecture-level vulnerabilities related to data propagation in the design phase [22]. Taint is used to represent the reach of an attack within a system. As shown in Fig. 1, taint starts at a taint source (node 1), which could be a component exposed to the external environment, then flows to connected components. Taint blockers (e.g. node 6) are secure components which prevent further propagation of taint. A system security property defines the set of critical components (e.g. node 7) which must remain free of taint after maximal propagation of taint through the system."
    }
