    {
      "text": "The paper addresses the challenge of automating and executing complex scientific data processing and co-simulation workflows involving multiple models developed in different programming languages and requiring different runtime environments, which are currently time-consuming, error-prone, and difficult to manage due to manual data transfer and configuration.",
      "original_text": "In order to perform complex data processing and co-simulation workflows for research on data driven energy systems, a generic, modular and highly scalable process operation framework is presented in this article. This framework consistently applies web technologies to build up a microservices architecture. It automates the startup, synchronization, and management of scientific data processing and simulation tools (e.g. Python, Matlab, OpenModelica) as part of larger transdisciplinary, multi-domain data processing and co-simulation workflows. It uses container virtualization on the underlying cluster computing environment to control and manage different simulation nodes. Within the frameworks processing workflow, software executables can be distributed to different nodes on the cluster, easily access data and communicate with other components via communication adapters and a high-performance messaging channel infrastructure. By integrating Apache NiFi, the framework also provides an easy-to-use web user interface to allow users to model, perform and operate workflows for future energy system solutions. As soon as a complex workflow is set up in the process operation framework, researchers can use the workflow without any setup or configuration on their local workstations and without knowing any details of the underlying infrastructure or software environment."
    },
    {
      "text": "The increasing complexity of energy system models requires modular and scalable model execution and automation of workflows involving many different models developed by specialists from various scientific disciplines, which leads to communication difficulties and the need for manual data transformation and configuration.",
      "original_text": "With the rapid development of hardware technology, modern hardware provides much more computational power to scientists than before and allows investigating more complex research questions by data processing and scientific simulation. The growing complexity causes an urgent need for modular and scalable model execution and automation of complex scientific simulation workflows with large amounts of data processing. Scientific simulation often requires the cooperation of specialists from many different scientific communities: electrical engineers, computer scientists, economists, physicists, chemists, biologists etc. Experts in these disciplines develop modules of subsystems for different parts of simulation research, which have to be integrated into a simulation environment and automatically run as a single coordinated simulation workflow. However, due to the increasing complexity of e.g. energy systems models (ESM) such simulations become more and more complex, making it almost impossible that the developer of a module in one field is the same person who develops another module in another field - which leads to communication difficulties. Furthermore, a simulation of energy systems may consist of many different models which are developed using various programming languages and tools. To connect these models to form a single co-simulation, developers need to implement yet more communication interfaces, which results in more work and, in some cases, exceeds their level of knowledge. Often, the acquired and transferred data in such models appears in different data formats, such as a mixture of measurement data, more general time series data, structured master data, unstructured text or binary assets. Not all modules use the raw output from the preceding modules, causing the need for manual transformation, which is prone to human error. Moreover, since models with specific dependencies and data sources can only be executed in their own runtime environments, complex model installation is very time-consuming. Therefore, the automation and execution of these modules as well as the efficient communication between them are challenging tasks."
